UP Health
Build Instructions & Developer Handbook
Version: 1.0
Status: Ready for Implementation
Companion Documents: UP_HEALTH_PROPOSAL_v2.md (what/why) ·
UP_HEALTH_BUILD_PLAN.md (phases/goals)
Builder: Solo Developer, $0 Budget
Date: June 28, 2026
How to Use This Document
This is the hands-on companion to the Build Plan. Where the Build Plan describes each
phase conceptually — what needs to exist and why — this handbook gives the concrete,
structural detail needed to actually start writing code: exact package names, file and folder
organization, naming conventions, required environment variables, and a checkable “done
when” for every step.
This document follows the same five phases as the Build Plan, in the same order, so the two
can be read side by side. Each step here corresponds to something described conceptually
in the Build Plan — if anything here seems to conflict with the Build Plan or the Proposal,
those two documents take precedence, and this handbook should be corrected to match
them.
Code snippets are intentionally avoided throughout this document. Where a very short
snippet is genuinely necessary to avoid ambiguity, it will be minimal — never a long block —
and everything else is described in plain, structural language instead.
Before Starting: Project-Wide Setup
Repository & Folder Structure
Set up the project as a single repository with this general shape at the top level:
A folder for the application source code itself (the exact framework-specific name

depends on the chosen frontend approach — e.g., an app or src folder, following
whatever convention the chosen framework uses by default; don’t fight the framework’s
own defaults here)
A docs folder containing this handbook, the Build Plan, and the Proposal, kept together
for reference but excluded from what gets deployed
A public or equivalent static-assets folder, if the framework provides one by default
A .env.local file for local environment variables (never committed)
A .env.example file documenting which environment variables are needed, without real
values (committed, safe to share)
A .gitignore file that excludes .env.local , dependency folders, and any build output
folders
This mirrors the same repository hygiene pattern used across this builder’s other LUKSO
projects — nothing unusual here, just consistency.
Environment Variables Needed
Based on everything specified in the Proposal and Build Plan, the following environment
variables should be anticipated from the start, even if some aren’t wired up until later
phases:
A LUKSO Mainnet RPC endpoint URL
The LUKSO Mainnet chain identifier
The LUKSO Mainnet Blockscout explorer’s base API URL (to be confirmed precisely
once reached in Phase 3)
Any identifier needed for the specific wallet-connection package chosen for the
standalone context, if that package requires one (to be confirmed once reached in
Phase 4)
Each of these should be documented in .env.example with a short comment explaining
what it’s for, even before its actual value is known, so the file stays an accurate map of what
the project needs.
Naming Conventions
Follow the same conventions already established in this builder’s other projects, for
consistency across all of them:
Components: PascalCase

Functions and variables: camelCase
Constants: UPPER_SNAKE_CASE
Files: match the convention of whatever they contain (PascalCase for component files,
camelCase or kebab-case for utility files, consistent with the framework’s own norms)
Git Discipline
Consistent with this builder’s established workflow: commit per meaningful unit of work, not
per phase. No commits should be made without being explicitly requested. No co-author
attribution should ever be added to commits.
PHASE 1: Foundation
Corresponds to Build Plan Phase 1. Read that section first for the “why” behind each item
below.
Step 1.1 — Scaffold the Project
Initialize a new frontend project using the framework approach described in the Proposal’s
Technical Approach section. At this stage, the goal is simply a project that runs locally and
shows a blank or placeholder page without errors.
Done when: the project starts locally without errors and shows something on screen,
however minimal.
Step 1.2 — Install Core Blockchain-Reading Capability
Add a library capable of reading arbitrary on-chain data from an EVM-compatible network.
This is the same category of library already used in this builder’s other projects for reading
contract state (the Proposal and this builder’s prior projects have used viem for this
purpose, and there’s no reason to deviate here — staying consistent reduces the number of
new tools being learned at once).
Done when: the library is installed and a basic connection to the LUKSO Mainnet RPC
endpoint can be established and confirmed (for example, successfully retrieving the current
block number as a smoke test — this is a standard, trivial call available in any such library,
useful purely to confirm the connection works before building anything real on top of it).
Step 1.3 — Add ERC725Y / LSP6 Data-Reading Capability
Add a library purpose-built for reading and decoding ERC725Y-structured data (the data

storage pattern that both LSP6 permissions and LSP3 metadata use). LUKSO publishes an
official library for exactly this purpose ( @erc725/erc725.js ), including the schema
definitions needed to correctly interpret LSP6 and LSP3 data keys without having to handroll that mapping. Use the official schema packages it provides rather than reconstructing
the key definitions manually.
Done when: given a known, real Universal Profile address, the raw AddressPermissions[]
array (the list of controller addresses) can be successfully retrieved and displayed in some
raw, unpolished form — a list of hex addresses on screen is a perfectly good sign of success
at this stage.
Step 1.4 — Address Validation & Non-LUKSO Input Handling
This is the addition flagged specifically in the Build Plan as not appearing in the Proposal.
Before attempting any permission lookup, the address entered by a user needs to pass
through a basic validation and existence check:
1. First, confirm the input is even a validly formatted blockchain address at all (correct
length and character format). If not, reject immediately with a plain message asking the
user to check the address they entered.
2. If correctly formatted, attempt the ERC725Y read from Step 1.3. If this read comes back
genuinely empty (no data at all associated with that address), this should be treated as
a normal, expected outcome — not an error — and communicated to the user as “no
Universal Profile or Vault was found at this address on LUKSO Mainnet,” rather than any
technical-sounding failure message.
3. Distinguish this “nothing found” case clearly, in the code’s own structure, from actual
technical failures (like the RPC endpoint being unreachable) — these need different
messages, and conflating them will confuse users in exactly the way the Build Plan
warns against.
Done when: all of the following behave distinctly and correctly:
A well-formed, real LUKSO address returns data
A well-formed but non-existent or non-LUKSO address (for example, a real Ethereum
address that has never touched LUKSO) returns a clear “not found” message, not an
error
A malformed address (wrong length, invalid characters) is rejected immediately, before
any network call is attempted
A genuine network failure (temporarily disconnect from the internet and try again, as a
manual test) produces a distinctly different message acknowledging a connection

problem, not a “not found” message
Phase 1 Checklist
Project scaffolded and runs locally
Blockchain-reading library installed and connected to LUKSO Mainnet RPC
ERC725Y/LSP6-reading library installed, using LUKSO’s official schemas
Raw controller list retrievable for a known real address
Malformed addresses rejected immediately, before any network call
Valid-but-empty addresses produce a clear “not found” message
Genuine network failures produce a distinctly different message than “not found”
PHASE 2: Permission Decoding
Corresponds to Build Plan Phase 2.
Step 2.1 — Enumerate All Controllers
Using the AddressPermissions[] array retrieved in Phase 1, loop through every address in
that list (not just the first one) and retrieve each one’s individual permission value, stored
under its own specific data key ( AddressPermissions:Permissions:<address> , per the
LSP6 schema).
Done when: given a profile with multiple controllers, every single one appears in the
retrieved data, not just the first.
Step 2.2 — Decode Each Permission Value
For each controller’s raw permission value, decode which specific capabilities it represents.
LUKSO’s official schema library from Step 1.3 already understands this bitfield structure —
the decoding work here is primarily about correctly using that library’s decoding functions
against each permission value, and organizing the result into a clear internal structure (for
example, a simple list of which named permissions are present for each controller) that the
next steps can build on.
Cross-reference the decoded output against LUKSO’s own published list of named
permissions directly, at least for a handful of test cases, rather than trusting the decoding
blindly — this is worth double-checking carefully once, since every later feature (flags
especially) depends on this being correct.

Step 2.3 — Translate to Plain Language
Build a simple mapping from each technical permission name to its plain-language
equivalent, as illustrated in the Proposal (for example, the technical permission allowing
arbitrary contract calls becomes “can call external contracts” in the displayed output). This
mapping is best kept as a single, centralized reference used everywhere permissions are
displayed, rather than repeated or reconstructed in multiple places — this keeps wording
consistent and makes future tone adjustments (raised as a concern in Build Plan Phase 5) a
one-place change rather than a hunt across the codebase.
Done when: a real controller’s permissions are shown as a readable list of plain-language
statements, matching the tone shown in the Proposal’s examples.
Step 2.4 — Distinguish Profiles from Vaults
Add a simple check to determine whether the address being examined is a Universal Profile
or an LSP9 Vault. Both standards support a standard interface-detection mechanism
(ERC165), which allows asking a contract directly which standards it supports, rather than
guessing from other clues. Use this to set a simple label distinguishing the two in the
display, without needing any separate decoding logic — the same permission-reading and
translation logic from Steps 2.1–2.3 applies identically to both.
Done when: looking up a known Vault address produces the same quality of permission
breakdown as a Profile, with a label correctly identifying it as a Vault.
Step 2.5 — Retrieve Basic Profile Metadata
Using the same ERC725Y-reading approach, retrieve the LSP3 metadata fields needed for
display purposes only: name and avatar image, where set. This is a straightforward read of a
different, well-documented data key, and should degrade gracefully (simply omitting the
name/avatar) if a profile hasn’t set this metadata, rather than treating a missing name or
avatar as any kind of error.
Done when: a profile with metadata set shows its name and avatar; a profile without
metadata set simply omits them without any error or broken layout.
Phase 2 Checklist
Every controller on a multi-controller profile is enumerated, not just one
Each controller’s permission value is decoded into named capabilities
Decoded output has been manually cross-checked against LUKSO’s published
permission list for at least a few real examples

Plain-language translations exist for every named permission, centralized in one place
Vault addresses are correctly distinguished from Profile addresses and produce equally
correct results
Basic metadata (name, avatar) displays when present and degrades gracefully when
absent
PHASE 3: Risk Analysis & Identity Gating
Corresponds to Build Plan Phase 3. This phase carries the most real-world consequence if
done carelessly — treat the identity-matching logic especially as its own well-tested piece,
exactly as the Build Plan emphasizes.
Step 3.1 — Basic Wallet Connection (Standalone Context, First Pass)
For this phase specifically, implement only a basic, single-context wallet connection
sufficient to know which address the user is connected as — the fuller dual-context
handling described in Build Plan Phase 4 is deliberately out of scope here. This connects to
the user’s Universal Profile Browser Extension the same way any standard web3 application
connects to an injected browser wallet.
Done when: connecting a browser wallet reveals the connected address somewhere in the
application’s internal state, even without any UI polish yet.
Step 3.2 — Identity-Matching Logic
Build a single, clearly named, centralized function whose only job is answering one question:
does the currently connected wallet address match the profile address currently being
examined? This should return a simple, unambiguous result and should be the only place in
the codebase that makes this determination — every feature in this phase that depends on
ownership (flags, staleness, next steps) should call this same function rather than reimplementing the comparison logic separately.
Address comparisons on EVM-compatible chains are case-insensitive in practice
(addresses are often displayed with mixed-case checksums, but represent the same
underlying address regardless of letter casing) — make sure the comparison logic accounts
for this, rather than comparing raw strings directly, which would incorrectly treat the same
address as a mismatch if the casing differs between where each value came from.
Done when, tested individually:
No wallet connected at all → treated as “not the owner”

Wallet connected, but viewing a different profile than the connected address → treated
as “not the owner”
Wallet connected, viewing the exact profile matching the connected address → treated
as “is the owner,” including when the two addresses differ only in letter casing
Step 3.3 — Contract-vs-Wallet Detection
For each controller address, check whether it has contract bytecode present or not (a
standard, simple check available directly through the blockchain-reading library from Phase
1 — checking if any code exists at a given address). This determines the simple “is this a
contract or a regular wallet” fact described in the Proposal, and requires no further logic
beyond that basic check for this release.
Done when: a known contract address correctly shows as a contract, and a known regular
wallet address correctly shows as a regular wallet.
Step 3.4 — Risk Flag Logic
Implement each of the three specific flag rules described in the Proposal’s Section 6.2, as
three separate, clearly named checks (keeping them separate rather than combined makes
each one easier to test and reason about independently):
Concentrated power check: a controller (other than what can be reasonably identified
as the profile’s main/original controller) holds the permission to add controllers or edit
permissions, and also holds broad execution or value-transfer permissions at the same
time.
No recovery path check: the total controller count for the profile is exactly one.
Suspicious delegate check: a Universal Receiver Delegate is set, and it points to an
address with no contract code present (or other simple markers of an
unusual/unverifiable delegate, kept intentionally simple for this release rather than
building deep contract analysis).
Each flag check should be a simple function that takes the already-decoded permission
data as input and returns whether that specific flag applies, plus the plain-language
explanation text to show if it does. Keep the explanation text next to the logic that triggers it,
rather than separated elsewhere, so the two stay in sync as wording gets refined during
Phase 5’s tone review.
Done when: each flag can be triggered individually against deliberately constructed test
scenarios (see Step 3.6), and does not trigger against a profile that shouldn’t match it.
Step 3.5 — Staleness / Last-Activity Retrieval

Using the LUKSO Mainnet Blockscout explorer’s public API, retrieve the timestamp of each
controller address’s most recent on-chain activity. Confirm the exact endpoint path against
the live explorer’s own API documentation at this point in the build (the Proposal notes this
should be verified at implementation time rather than assumed) — Blockscout’s API
conventions are consistent across its deployments on other networks, which gives a
reasonable starting expectation, but the specific path should be checked directly against
LUKSO’s own instance before relying on it.
This data should only be fetched and displayed once Step 3.2’s identity check confirms the
viewer is the profile’s own owner, consistent with the rest of this phase’s sensitive
information.
Done when: a real controller’s last-activity timestamp is retrieved and displayed only when
viewing as the verified owner, and is absent otherwise.
Step 3.6 — Constructing Test Scenarios
Since real user profiles shouldn’t be relied on for testing risky configurations, this step is
about deliberately setting up a small number of test profiles (on Mainnet, using addresses
controlled directly for testing purposes, with minimal real value involved) that intentionally
match each flag condition, plus at least one that intentionally matches none of them. This
gives a reliable, repeatable way to confirm flag logic continues working correctly as the rest
of the build progresses, rather than relying on hoping a real profile happens to demonstrate
the right conditions.
Done when: a small set of test profiles exists, collectively covering each flag condition
individually and a clean “no flags” case, and can be re-checked quickly at any point during
later phases.
Step 3.7 — One-Line Summary Generation
Once flags are available, build a simple function that counts triggered flags and controllers,
and produces the single summary sentence described in the Proposal’s Section 6.6. This is
a presentation step layered on top of already-computed data, not a new data source.
Step 3.8 — Actionable Next Steps
For each flag defined in Step 3.4, attach a short, concrete suggested action and a link to the
existing LSP6 permission manager tool already available in the ecosystem (via lukso.tools).
Keep this paired directly with each flag’s definition, in the same place, for the same reason
given in Step 3.4 — keeping related pieces together as wording gets refined later.
Phase 3 Checklist

Basic wallet connection reveals the connected address
Identity-matching logic is centralized in one function, used everywhere ownership
matters
Identity matching correctly handles address casing differences
All three identity-matching scenarios (no wallet / wrong profile / correct profile) tested
individually and confirmed correct
Contract-vs-wallet detection works correctly for known examples of each
All three risk flags implemented as separate, independently testable checks
Staleness data only appears for verified owners
A small set of deliberately constructed test profiles exists, covering each flag plus a
clean case
One-line summary correctly reflects flag and controller counts
Every flag has an attached next step and correct link to the external permission manager
PHASE 4: Interface & Dual-Context Support
Corresponds to Build Plan Phase 4.
Step 4.1 — Assemble the Full Results Display
Bring together the outputs of Phases 2 and 3 into the actual layout described conceptually
in the Build Plan: summary line at top, controller-by-controller breakdown beneath it, and
flags with next steps shown where applicable. This is primarily a layout and visual-design
task at this point — the underlying data and logic already exist from prior phases.
Step 4.2 — Context Detection
Implement a check, run once when the application loads, determining whether the
application is currently running inside a Grid’s iframe (a parent page context is present) or
being visited directly with no such parent context. This determines which of the two
connection paths below gets used.
Step 4.3 — Grid-Embedded Connection Path
Add LUKSO’s official package built specifically for Mini-App/Grid contexts ( @lukso/upprovider ), following LUKSO’s own official Mini-App templates as the reference pattern for

how this is conventionally wired up (LUKSO publishes reference templates for exactly this
purpose — worth reviewing one directly as a structural reference rather than reconstructing
the pattern from scratch). This handles both identifying whose Grid the application is sitting
in, and allowing a visitor to connect with one click.
Step 4.4 — Standalone Connection Path
For the case where Step 4.2 detects no Grid context, connect using the standard approach
of detecting and connecting to the user’s injected browser wallet extension directly — the
same general pattern used by typical web3 applications, and the same pattern LUKSO’s
own standalone-app examples follow.
Step 4.5 — Converge Both Paths
Regardless of which path was used in Step 4.3 or 4.4, both should ultimately produce the
same simple result: a connected address, fed into the exact same identity-matching logic
built in Phase 3, Step 3.2. Structure the code so that everything past “we now have a
connected address” is shared, common logic — not duplicated separately for each
connection path. This is worth being deliberate about structurally, since the Build Plan
specifically warns against accidentally building what amounts to two separate applications.
Step 4.6 — Standalone Manual Address Input
Ensure the manual address-paste flow built in Phase 1 remains available and prominent in
the standalone context specifically, independent of connecting a wallet — since the tool’s
basic promise (anyone can look up basic information for any address without connecting
anything) should remain true here.
Phase 4 Checklist
Full results display assembled and readable
Context detection correctly identifies Grid-embedded vs. standalone at load time
Grid-embedded connection tested for real, inside an actual test Grid (not simulated
locally)
Standalone connection tested for real, visiting the deployed URL directly outside any
Grid
Both connection paths converge on the same identity-matching logic from Phase 3
Manual address input remains available and functional in the standalone context

PHASE 5: Polish & Launch Readiness
Corresponds to Build Plan Phase 5.
Step 5.1 — Deliberate Edge-Case Pass
Working through this deliberately rather than waiting for issues to surface naturally,
manually test: a temporarily broken network connection, a profile with an unusually large
number of controllers, a Universal Receiver Delegate pointing to an address that no longer
has any code at all, and a profile with zero controllers at all if that’s even a reachable state.
Each should produce a calm, clear message — revisit Phase 1’s error-handling structure if
any of these produce a confusing result instead.
Step 5.2 — Tone Review
Read through every flag explanation, every message, and the one-line summary logic as if
encountering it for the first time with no prior context. Since Step 2.3 and Step 3.4
deliberately centralized this wording in a small number of places, this review should be
quick to perform and quick to adjust.
Step 5.3 — Cross-Context Final Pass
With the application actually deployed (not just running locally), test the complete flow once
more in both contexts side by side: as a standalone visit, and embedded in a real test Grid,
confirming both feel equally complete and polished.
Step 5.4 — Success Criteria Verification
Go through the Proposal’s Section 12 success criteria one by one, confirming each is
genuinely met, not just assumed to be from earlier testing.
Step 5.5 — Share
Share the deployed tool with the LUKSO community as described in the Proposal, and begin
tracking any feedback received as input toward the Phase 2 ideas already identified and
deliberately deferred in the Proposal’s Section 8.
Phase 5 Checklist
All deliberate edge cases tested and produce calm, clear messages
All flag and message wording reviewed fresh, with no alarmist or accusatory phrasing
found
Full flow re-tested on the actual deployed version, in both contexts

Every item in the Proposal’s Section 12 success criteria confirmed individually
Tool shared publicly with the LUKSO community
